[{:db/id "q-38293/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "70087bf9-e860-570f-9dde-409a5651d33a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38293/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "b63728da-f7ac-58fb-9909-cfdfa6096ed6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38293/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "43ce1fdf-8e8a-54cb-b634-270a6857697a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38293/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d07b4d3a-0a17-5f2c-a16c-b0b451b8dc12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38293/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "888c2c61-5acb-5360-8c19-a73b3edefb26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38293/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f7a183-39dd-5980-9f62-bb7338d90592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-38293/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-38293/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-38293/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-38293/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-38293/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-38293/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-38293" :question/id #uuid "95a80834-eafd-54c7-aa21-4bf9e18db155" :question/math-academy-id "q-38293" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6$ has one of its roots in the interval $(0,3),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 6\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,3),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=1,-2,3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n1-2+3=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-38293/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38293"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-323218/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "3db7807c-da93-5f6f-8bf5-832fec41bb95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323218/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f0f861b-9518-5001-9a35-06d48a9f94df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323218/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-323218/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323218" :question/id #uuid "3f82cc12-d956-5296-9d23-6bdadb185052" :question/math-academy-id "q-323218" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-7$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-323218/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-323218"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-323819/field-1/b78cefa051ec62e42babb0396ce415bc1c6b03c29d281d68224ab0312a503d34" :answer/id #uuid "d653916d-5138-5616-a1d4-14509ffa1ea1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323819/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d03e1aea-f397-5196-9173-82319cdcecfd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323819/field-1/b78cefa051ec62e42babb0396ce415bc1c6b03c29d281d68224ab0312a503d34" :answer-field/choices ["q-323819/field-1/b78cefa051ec62e42babb0396ce415bc1c6b03c29d281d68224ab0312a503d34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323819/field-2/b7e1c893089e9c7b8066a199c43cdff517fe004f1db62d878596274884ad36ec" :answer/id #uuid "7d4146ea-ef74-54fb-b8a7-8f7978b7e5e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323819/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b61613f-414a-5f86-b6d2-5d1181143f46" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323819/field-2/b7e1c893089e9c7b8066a199c43cdff517fe004f1db62d878596274884ad36ec" :answer-field/choices ["q-323819/field-2/b7e1c893089e9c7b8066a199c43cdff517fe004f1db62d878596274884ad36ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323819/field-3/73eeaf3fae024b54005d89a5185040ad67cf4b6c72d401d6ad32bf0e79c13de9" :answer/id #uuid "09ce3642-5223-5ab2-bc23-63df075b970e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323819/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "0bd080ed-486a-56f7-a976-1b1e5c9c2610" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323819/field-3/73eeaf3fae024b54005d89a5185040ad67cf4b6c72d401d6ad32bf0e79c13de9" :answer-field/choices ["q-323819/field-3/73eeaf3fae024b54005d89a5185040ad67cf4b6c72d401d6ad32bf0e79c13de9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323819" :question/id #uuid "9930c420-f372-5a27-8b7c-8f38c6bfb53a" :question/math-academy-id "q-323819" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-9x-21$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-3,-2).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-21$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-21}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 21,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-3,-2)$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{7}{3}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-9$ | $-21$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $21$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-\\frac{7}{3},$ $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-323819/field-1" "q-323819/field-2" "q-323819/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-323819"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
